315 DEVELOPING POTENTIAL SOURCES OF INCOME (AAM)

SR 11-27 Dated 07/11 Previous Policy

_______________________________________________________________

Developing potential sources of income means that as a condition of eligibility for assistance, if an applicant or recipient is financially eligible for adult category financial or medical assistance, the individual must explore and apply for all potential sources of income or benefits available to the individual including, but not limited to:

. Supplemental Security Income (SSI) described under Title XVI of the Social Security Act;

Exception : Individuals requesting medical assistance-only do not have to apply for SSI benefits. However, encourage individuals to apply.

. all Social Security Administration (SSA)-related benefits, including Social Security disability insurance (SSDI) program benefits described under Title II of the Social Security Act;

. Veterans benefits, including the Veteran ' s Affairs Aid and Attendance Allowance;

Exception: if the individual is incapable of applying for the aid and attendance allowance, does not have an authorized representative to apply on the individual ' s behalf, and the nursing facility will not apply on the individual ' s behalf, the Family Services Specialist must initiate the application for the aid and attendance allowance on the individual ' s behalf.

. Retirement benefits or pensions;

. Disability benefits or pensions;

. Unemployment or worker ' s compensation;

. Contributions from any liable third party; and

. Third-party medical coverage.

315.11 Denial or Termination of Assistance (AAM)

SR 13-12 Dated 07/13 Previous Policy

_____________________________________________________________

Deny or terminate financial assistance for the entire assistance group (AG) and deny or terminate medical assistance for all adults in the AG if any individual in the AG who is potentially eligible for other income or benefits refuses or fails to:

. apply or complete the application process for the other income or benefit(s);

. provide information or verification to obtain the other income or benefit(s);

. cooperate with the eligibility-determining agencies or individuals responsible for the other income or benefit(s);

. meet the application timeframes described Section 315.03 , Application Requirements for Developing Potential Sources of Income , or set by the eligibility-determining agencies or individuals responsible for the other income or benefit(s);

. pursue all appeal options, up to, but not including, court action, if found ineligible for the other income or benefit due to medical reasons; or

. accept the other income or benefit(s) for which they may be eligible.

Deny or terminate Aid to the Permanently and Totally Disabled (APTD) financial assistance when:

. an individual applies for an APTD cash grant on or after July 1, 2011 and has been denied Social Security Administration (SSA)-related benefits due to not meeting SSA medical disability criteria within 12 months prior to the individual's application date, or any time after the individual's application date. Eligibility for APTD medical assistance remains unchanged;

. an individual's application for an APTD cash grant is pending on or after July 1, 2011 and the individual has been denied SSA-related benefits due to not meeting SSA medical disability criteria within 12 months prior to the individual's application date, or any time after the individual's application date. Eligibility for APTD medical assistance remains unchanged;

. an individual was receiving an APTD cash grant as of June 30, 2011 and has been denied Social Security Administration (SSA)-related benefits for a second time due to not meeting SSA medical disability criteria, as long as the following criteria were met:

- only one denial from prior to July 1, 2011 may be used, as long as that denial occurred between the 12 months prior to the individual's application date and June 30, 2011, and

- at least one denial must have occurred any time after July 1, 2011. Note : If no denials occurred between the 12 months prior to the individual's application date and June 30, 2011, then both denials must come on or after the July 1, 2011 date before APTD cash may be terminated.

Eligibility for APTD medical assistance remains unchanged; or

. any APTD financial assistance applicant or recipient does not provide the required proof from SSA within timeframes

If the Department has denied or terminated an individual ' s APTD cash grant due to the individual not meeting the SSA medical criteria, or because the individual did not give the Department the required verification timely, the individual may reapply for APTD cash, BUT they are to be denied until the individual provides the proof described in Section 315.09 , APTD Verification Requirements for SSA Denial .

317.03 Reimbursement - Medical Assistance for Non-Institutionalized Individuals (AAM)

SR 11-43 Dated 11/11 Previous Policy

__________________________________________________________________

Inform all non-institutionalized applicants for medical assistance under the OAA, ANB, MEAD, BCCP, and APTD programs:

. of DHHS ' s obligation to recover from their estate for medical assistance received by recipients age 55 and older;

. that for the purposes of recovery of medical assistance, a recipient ' s "estate" includes:

- all property, real or personal, in which the recipient holds an interest on the date of the recipient ' s death. Such interests include joint tenancy with rights of survivorship, tenancy in common, life estates, and living trusts, without regard to the date that such title or interest was established; and

- both assets that pass through probate and assets that pass outside the probate process, such as certain trust assets, assets held jointly, and unpaid annuity balances that benefit the recipient;

. that DHHS will only pursue recovery from the recipient ' s assets;

. that DHHS will only pursue recovery after the recipient ' s death, unless the property is voluntarily sold or refinanced while a lien is in place;

. that if DHHS ' s pursuit of recovery would cause undue hardship to the recipient ' s heirs, a waiver is available when recovery action begins;

. that DHHS will pursue recovery from the recipient ' s estate, but only if the recipient has no surviving spouse, child who is under age 21 years, or child who is blind or permanently and totally disabled; and

. that recovery is limited to the actual amount paid on behalf of the recipient from age 55 and older.

Exception : If benefits were incorrectly paid, recoupment of the erroneous payment can be required while the recipient is alive or regardless of the above criteria for recovery. See PART 633 , RECOUPMENT OF MEDICAL ASSISTANCE .

Inform the individual or the ANB child ' s parents of DHHS ' s recovery obligations and the recipient ' s rights and responsibilities with DFA Form 770, Reimbursement Agreement and Acknowledgment . Failure or refusal to complete the medical assistance portion of DFA Form 770 does not result in denial or termination from assistance.

*Scan the original signed DFA Form 770 *into the individual ' s electronic case record.

SELF-EMPLOYMENT (AAM)

SR 04-27 Dated 10/04 Previous Policy

____________________________________________________________

Self-employment income:

. is cash - or dollar value of in-kind benefits - received by an individual, which is earned directly from the individual ' s own business, trade, or profession;

. includes the total gain from the sale of any capital goods or equipment related to the business, trade, or profession; and

. is not subject to Federal Income Tax (FIT) or Medicare Tax (FICA) withholding.

Treatment : Earned Income

Average self-employment income over the period of time the income is intended to cover.

Subtract the monthly costs of doing business from the average gross monthly self-employment income. If the total cost of doing business equals or exceeds the gross self-employment income, there is no countable earned income.

The costs of doing business are the expenses that are directly related to producing the goods or services without which the goods or services could not be produced.

Allowable costs of doing business:

. identifiable costs of labor, stock, and raw materials needed to perform the job such as lumber and demonstration products;

. tools and equipment;

. work-related clothing;

. business-related vehicle expenses and transportation costs incurred while on the job, including cost of maintaining the vehicle, mileage, tokens or tolls, gasoline, insurance, but not transportation expenses incurred going to and from work;

. payments on the principal and interest of the purchase price of income-producing real estate and capital assets, equipment, machinery, and other durable goods;

. advertising expenses;

. postage;

. cost of maintaining a job site, including a room in one ' s home;

. telephone expenses;

. electricity for the job site;

. bookkeeping and payroll expenses;

. employees;

. lodging away from the home while on a job;

. rentals related to being able to complete a job, whether it is equipment or another type of rental;

. insurance premiums on equipment and real estate used in producing income;

. insurance premiums including disability, workers compensation, job site insurance, and bonding;

. employee taxes;

. taxes on income-producing property, business profits taxes, and other mandatory business-related taxes (but not federal, state, or local income taxes); and

. any other identifiable cost unique to a particular self-employment business.

The following expenses are not allowable as a cost of doing business:

. depreciation;

. net losses from previous periods;

. money set aside for retirement purposes;

. personal business and entertainment expenses;

. personal transportation;

. any amount that exceeds the payment a household receives from a boarder for lodging and meals;

. purchase price of capital assets (such as real estate, machinery, and equipment); and

. personal (not business-related) taxes such as income taxes.

See Boarder, Child Care , Roomer and Rental Income for the treatment of those types of self-employment income.

625.05 Allowable Medical Expenses (AAM)

SR 00-12 Dated 10/00 Previous Policy

____________________________________________________________

Offset the monthly or six-month spenddown by incurred costs of medical care and services recognized as legitimate under state law. Incurred costs for medical care and services that the medical assistance program does not cover are also considered valid expenses. Allow the medical expenses of the assistance group and any individual the recipient is liable for.

Incurred cost means the amount of the medical expense which the individual is responsible for after third-party payments have been made.

Exception: Allow medical expenses paid by local, county, state, or federal public funds, including medical expense payments using local, county, state, or federal public funds, provided by agencies that have a contract with the State to provide these payments (such as a Community Mental Health Center or Area Agency) as if the individual were responsible for them.

The following are examples of allowable incurred medical expenses:

• current medical expenses;

• health insurance premiums such as Blue Cross-Blue Shield, Medicare Part B, and any other enrollment fees or premiums;

• over-the-counter medication that is part of a treatment plan supervised by a physician;

• medical transportation (see Chapter 900 of the Medical Assistance Manual );

• adult medical day care; and

• currently obligated unpaid prior medical bills, including debts of individuals for whom the recipient is liable.
